<!--
    structsynth: a reader whose own layout leaves some accessed bytes unclaimed
    is not answered by a structure that puts a member boundary inside them.

    Source shape (gcc -O2, endbr64 dropped):

        struct T { int i0; char c4, c5, c6, c7, c8, c9, c10, c11;
                   long pad; long l10; char *s; long l20; };
        void full(struct T *p, unsigned v, char *s) {          // @0x100000
            p->i0 = 5; p->c7 = v; p->c8 = v >> 3; p->c9 = v >> 5;
            p->c10 = v >> 7; p->l10 = 0; p->s = s; p->l20 = v;
        }
        unsigned long rd_c8(struct T *p, char *s, unsigned w) { // @0x100040
            unsigned v; memcpy(&v, &p->c7, 4);   // 4-byte load at 7
            p->c8 = w >> 3;                      // byte store at 8
            p->i0 = 5; p->l10 = 0; p->s = s; p->l20 = v;
            return v * 3u;
        }
        rd_c9 @0x100080 and rd_c10 @0x1000c0 store at 9 and 10 instead.

        full                                  rd_c8
        89 f0              mov eax,esi         8b 4f 07     mov ecx,[rdi+7]
        c7 07 05 00 00 00  mov [rdi],5         c1 ea 03     shr edx,3
        c1 e8 03           shr eax,3           48 89 77 20  mov [rdi+0x20],rsi
        40 88 77 07        mov [rdi+7],sil     88 57 08     mov [rdi+8],dl
        88 47 08           mov [rdi+8],al      c7 07 05 .. mov [rdi],5
        ...                                    8d 04 49     lea eax,[rcx+rcx*2]
        88 47 09 / 88 47 0a  bytes at 9, 10    48 c7 47 18  mov [rdi+0x18],0
        48 89 47 28        mov [rdi+0x28],rax  48 89 4f 28  mov [rdi+0x28],rcx
        c3                 ret                 c3           ret

    The load at 7 is unaligned, so no reader claims a field for it and its
    own layout spells it through filler, as *(uint4 *)&a0->field_0x4[3]. The
    engine decides whether a load may print after a store by comparing their
    addresses, and it takes one base plus two different constants to be two
    different objects whatever the access widths. The filler spelling is not
    one base plus a constant, so the load keeps its own statement ahead of the
    store. full's structure, which contains every field each reader claims,
    has a char at 7: answered with it, the load is spelled through
    a0->field_0x7, is judged distinct from a0->field_0x8, and prints after the
    store that overwrites its second byte.

    All asserts run with option structsynth param, the setting this rule is
    part of. full mints struct_0 and each reader keeps its own shape.
-->
